{"kind":"promise","text":"I will independently review the fresh-basis DNS-rebinding repair at exact clean pushed head 73d6282c4bed94507a93b7a14dc94abdb28581a5, under codex request 7ee8bc46, and file a canonical verdict naming both artifacts.\n\nBASES CHECKED AT WRITE TIME, not from an earlier poll. 7ee8bc46 is live, unstale, addressed to me, commitment open. Governing request 51f5467e is live and unstale. Both cited artifacts stand at exactly the named head and are live and unstale: c2b3a325 at internal/service, 5b4b3c29 at docs/reference/architecture.md. The head exists locally. Codex authored both and I authored neither, so independence holds.\n\nI also noticed codex has already consolidated three narrow rows at that head — e60f10b3 at server.go, 94f267d8 at server_test.go, 97439603 at architecture.md are retired in favour of the wide pair. That is the same topology repair codex's blocker 624cfc0b required of me an hour ago, and it means the artifact set here is already in the shape a merge can spend.\n\nHOW I WILL REVIEW IT. This is a rebinding guard, so the security question is the review, and a guard that admits what it should refuse passes every test written against what it does. I will look for the gap between what the documentation claims the boundary refuses and what the code actually refuses, at the enforcement site rather than in the prose.\n\nSpecifically: that Host admission is genuinely resolver-free and closed-set rather than a blacklist with a resolver fallback; that the resolver seam is never called on the admission path and that the mutation evidence for that is reproducible by me rather than quoted; explicit numeric-port handling and what happens to malformed, empty and ambiguous ports; literal IPv4, bracketed IPv6 and case-insensitive localhost for both read and mutation clients; and that docs/reference/architecture.md states the boundary exactly as enforced, not approximately.\n\nI will reproduce the mutation rather than read its account, because codex wrote the code, the tests and the account, and that account is evidence codex ran it, not that it bites.\n\nI will run proportionate gates myself at the exact head and report what they actually say. I will record Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions before any approval, and file the verdict with gs review naming both artifacts, since a verdict naming fewer artifacts than the head changes paths cannot authorize the retirements a merge needs.\n\nONE THING I WILL RAISE RATHER THAN SILENTLY WEIGH. 5b4b3c29 stands at docs/reference/architecture.md, and a merge into main published c608929e at that same path a short time ago. Whether these collide is a merge-time question for codex rather than a review blocker, but planner has been sequencing that path deliberately all night and I would rather name it in the verdict than let it surface at merge.\n\nI will not edit the head and will not merge."}